<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The hosted console's Windows end, running inside the prefix.

    Why this project exists: the game's console lives in a Windows process under
    Proton, and the Windows launcher's console tap is the parent end of two named
    pipes that only a Windows process can create. A native Linux launcher cannot
    open a Wine kernel object, and it cannot read the game's own streams either —
    `proton run` discards them (measured three ways; see Linux.Core/HostedConsole).

    So this process does what the Windows launcher did: it creates the two pipe
    ends the game's console setup expects (`R5F_CONSOLE_PIPE`/`R5F_CONSOLE_IN`)
    and bridges them to the launcher over loopback TCP, which Proton does not
    namespace and therefore crosses freely.

    It references R5Flowstate.Spawn and uses its HostedConsoleTap verbatim. That
    is the point: the game-facing half — the pipe names, the line rules, the
    512-character command cap — is upstream's own code, not a re-implementation
    that could drift from it.

    Target: win-x64, self-contained, trimmed, and NOT single-file, exactly like
    the art host (a single-file exe unpacks itself into a temp directory on every
    run, which is a needless thing to ask Proton to do). install.sh publishes this
    project into winhost/r5f-relay/ — a folder of its own, never the art host's:
    two trimmed self-contained publishes in one folder overwrite each other's
    framework, and the app that published first then dies at startup.

    OutputType is WinExe so no console window is allocated — subsystem 2 in the PE
    optional header rather than 3 — because one of these runs per role per launch
    and the player saw a dark Wine console appear for each. The relay's own trace
    (<c>Relay.Say</c>) already writes its stdout inside a try, so a process with no
    console handle loses a diary entry and nothing else. Nothing on this side waits
    on that stream: readiness is the loopback accept.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <PublishSingleFile>false</PublishSingleFile>
    <AssemblyName>r5f-relay</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>R5Flowstate.ConsoleRelay</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <Version>2.0.0</Version>
    <InformationalVersion>Linux-2.00</InformationalVersion>
    <!-- Nothing here is reached by reflection; partial rather than full because
         the console's own code is shared with the Windows launcher's assembly. -->
    <TrimMode>partial</TrimMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\R5Flowstate.Spawn\R5Flowstate.Spawn.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
